dropbear: no SSH login as root without a password, except in failsafe

OpenWrt ships dropbear patched so that root logs in over SSH with no password at all while
root has none (600-allow-blank-root-password.patch: the "none" auth method succeeds for root
whatever -B says). A fresh image has no root password, so until someone sets one, anything that
can reach port 22 - a LAN device, or a tunnel that lands on the router - gets a root shell
without being asked anything. LuCI at least shows a "no password set" warning; SSH shows none.

That patch is removed. dropbear then refuses a blank password on every method (2025.89: the
password method rejects a blank hash unconditionally, and the "none" method needs -B), so SSH
works as soon as a root password is set, in LuCI or with passwd, with no restart. Key logins
are unaffected.

Failsafe keeps its blank-password shell: it starts dropbear itself (dropbear.failsafe), root
has no password there by design, and it is the recovery path when the password is lost. That
call gains -B, which allows exactly the blank-password login the removed patch allowed.

Applies to: OpenWrt v25.12.5, package/network/services/dropbear.
Upstream status: local to CleanWRT (upstream keeps blank root logins on purpose).

diff --git a/package/network/services/dropbear/files/dropbear.failsafe b/package/network/services/dropbear/files/dropbear.failsafe
index 417265b..9003166 100755
--- a/package/network/services/dropbear/files/dropbear.failsafe
+++ b/package/network/services/dropbear/files/dropbear.failsafe
@@ -55,7 +55,7 @@ failsafe_dropbear () {
 		return 1
 	}
 
-	_dropbear ${kargs}
+	_dropbear -B ${kargs}
 }
 
 boot_hook_add failsafe failsafe_dropbear
diff --git a/package/network/services/dropbear/patches/600-allow-blank-root-password.patch b/package/network/services/dropbear/patches/600-allow-blank-root-password.patch
deleted file mode 100644
